# Project information architecture

A visitor should learn what the system is, what Ritvik did, and what is still open before the part numbers and the journal.

The homepage is out of scope. Do not change `src/app/page.tsx`, `HomeHero`, `HomeHeroIntro`, or the homepage project bands to follow this structure.

## Order on a project page

1. Overview. Title, what the system is for, his role, status, and one strong photo or clip.
2. How it works. A diagram, an annotated photo, or a short signal or power path.
3. His work. Only the subsystems he actually touched. Team work stays labeled as team work.
4. One or two debugging stories. What he saw, what he measured, what he changed, what happened.
5. What is demonstrated, and what is not finished.
6. Bench notes. Timelines, revisions, schedule notes, and reflections stay on the page inside a collapsed section. Do not delete them.

Skip a section when the project does not have that kind of work. Do not invent a mechanical section for a compiler.

## Cards and routes

- Keep existing routes and the original project title.
- A directory card can name the organization and use a longer summary. The homepage card keeps the existing `oneLine`.
- Group the projects directory by engineering relevance. Do not regroup the homepage arrays.

## Experiences and research

- Say what the organization builds, then what he did, then link the project.
- A preprint stays a preprint. A review stays a review.
